WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.320
MCP servers can expose more than tools. Resources are one of the three core MCP primitives,

2
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:11.840
and they give host applications a structured way to pull data from a server and inject it

3
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:18.559
into model context. Coming up in this video, we'll cover how resources are defined, what URI

4
00:00:18.559 --> 00:00:26.079
schemes and MIME types do, and the difference between static and dynamic content. A tool is

5
00:00:26.079 --> 00:00:30.959
something the model reaches for. A resource is something the application hands over,

6
00:00:30.959 --> 00:00:38.159
and that difference drives everything else about how resources behave. An MCP resource is a URI

7
00:00:38.159 --> 00:00:45.040
addressable data object that a server exposes so the host application can inject it into context.

8
00:00:45.040 --> 00:00:51.599
It's application controlled, which means the host decides what gets pulled in, not the model. Each

9
00:00:51.599 --> 00:00:59.200
resource is reached through a URI scheme, the protocol prefix-like file, DB or config, that

10
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:05.279
tells a client how to interpret the address. And each one carries a MIME type, a standard label

11
00:01:05.279 --> 00:01:12.080
like text slash plain, or application slash JSON that declares the format of the content coming

12
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:18.400
back. Together, they form the contract between the server that owns the data and the host that

13
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:25.199
decides when to use it. A resource definition has only two required fields. The URI is the

14
00:01:25.199 --> 00:01:30.800
stable address a client uses to request that resource, and the name is a human readable label

15
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:36.959
that identifies it. Those two are all the spec demands. The MIME type is optional. When you

16
00:01:36.959 --> 00:01:42.639
include it, it helps the host interpret the content it gets back, telling it whether to treat the

17
00:01:42.639 --> 00:01:49.360
payload as plain text, JSON, or something binary. But the resource is still perfectly retrievable

18
00:01:49.360 --> 00:01:56.720
without one. Behind the scenes, a content handler on the server produces or fetches the data when a

19
00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:04.800
client calls resources slash read. So think of URI and name as the contract and MIME type as helpful

20
00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:11.839
metadata that makes the returned content easier for the host to handle correctly. The most important

21
00:02:11.839 --> 00:02:17.759
design decision for any resource is whether it's static or dynamic. Static resources hold content

22
00:02:17.759 --> 00:02:24.479
that doesn't change between requests. Configuration files, fixed reference tables, and policy documents

23
00:02:24.479 --> 00:02:30.639
are typical examples. Because they're stable, they can be cached safely and don't require a live

24
00:02:30.639 --> 00:02:36.639
handler to run on every call. Dynamic resources are the opposite. They're generated or fetched at

25
00:02:36.639 --> 00:02:43.839
request time, so the content can differ from one call to the next. Database query results, live API

26
00:02:43.839 --> 00:02:50.320
responses, and user-specific data all fall into this category. Caching a dynamic resource without

27
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:55.679
an expiry policy is a common mistake that causes models to work with stale context.

28
00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:02.639
URI scheme design matters more than it might seem. A well-designed scheme is self-describing

29
00:03:02.639 --> 00:03:10.800
and domain consistent. Using db colon slash slash for database records, file colon slash slash for

30
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:17.039
file system content, and config colon slash slash for configuration snapshots makes the source of a

31
00:03:17.039 --> 00:03:23.520
resource obvious from the address alone. Stability is equally important. If a URI changes between

32
00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:29.679
server versions, any client logic that cached the old address breaks silently. You should also avoid

33
00:03:29.679 --> 00:03:37.600
embedding volatile state, like session tokens or timestamps, directly into the URI path. Stable URIs

34
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:43.440
let host applications build resource injection rules once and rely on them across deployments

35
00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:49.119
without ongoing maintenance overhead. A consistent scheme also makes it easier to write access control

36
00:03:49.119 --> 00:03:56.479
rules that apply across all resources of the same type. Here's a practical problem. A single resource

37
00:03:56.479 --> 00:04:03.279
entry points at one fixed URI, but a server often holds thousands of files or records. You can't list

38
00:04:03.279 --> 00:04:10.160
them all in advance. That's what resource templates solve. A template exposes a parameterized URI with

39
00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:16.559
a named placeholder written in the standard URI template syntax, so file colon slash slash slash

40
00:04:16.559 --> 00:04:22.320
open brace path, close brace stands in for any path. The client discovers these templates through

41
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:28.320
a distinct call, resources slash templates slash list, which is separate from the plain resources

42
00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:34.640
slash list. Once the host knows the template, it fills in the parameter, say the actual file path,

43
00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:40.880
and then calls resources slash read on that concrete URI. So templates give you a compact

44
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.320
way to advertise a whole family of addressable resources without enumerating everyone up front.

45
00:04:47.279 --> 00:04:54.160
There are three resource types you'll encounter most often in MCP server designs. File resources

46
00:04:54.160 --> 00:05:01.519
use the file colon slash slash scheme and return plain text or binary content with a content type

47
00:05:01.519 --> 00:05:08.799
of text slash plain or application slash octet stream. Database records use a db colon slash

48
00:05:08.799 --> 00:05:15.760
slash scheme and return query results as JSON with a content type of application slash JSON.

49
00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:23.679
Config snapshots use config colon slash slash and return structured key value content from a

50
00:05:23.679 --> 00:05:29.600
configuration store. Each type pairs a scheme with a predictable MIME type, which makes it

51
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:35.760
straightforward for host applications to route the content to the right part of the model's context

52
00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:42.799
without inspecting the payload on every call. MCP supports resource subscriptions, which solve a

53
00:05:42.799 --> 00:05:48.160
real problem with dynamic content. Without subscriptions, the only way to keep a resource

54
00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:54.959
current is to pull, fetch it on every request, and hope the version in context is fresh. With

55
00:05:54.959 --> 00:06:01.600
subscriptions, a client registers interest in a specific URI after the first retrieval. When the

56
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:07.760
server detects that resource's content has changed, it sends a notification to the subscribed client.

57
00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:13.679
The client then refetches the updated content using the same URI. This pattern keeps dynamic

58
00:06:13.679 --> 00:06:19.440
resources fresh without unnecessary polling and without flooding the server with repeat read calls

59
00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:26.399
when nothing has changed. Exposing a resource from a server involves three implementation steps.

60
00:06:26.959 --> 00:06:32.480
First, you declare the resource in the server's resource list at startup so clients can discover

61
00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:40.239
it when they call resources slash list. Second, you implement a read handler that accepts a URI

62
00:06:40.239 --> 00:06:46.559
and returns the resource content, along with the appropriate MIME type. Third, if the resource is

63
00:06:46.559 --> 00:06:52.480
dynamic and change notifications matter for your use case, you implement a subscribe handler that

64
00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:58.559
manages client registrations. The host application orchestrates all of this by calling resources

65
00:06:58.559 --> 00:07:05.440
slash list to see what's available and resources slash read to retrieve specific content when it

66
00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:12.399
decides to inject it. The application controlled nature of resources is the distinction people most

67
00:07:12.399 --> 00:07:18.399
often get backwards. The host application decides which resources to retrieve and when to inject

68
00:07:18.399 --> 00:07:23.679
them into context. The model doesn't invoke resources the way it invokes tools. Once the

69
00:07:23.679 --> 00:07:28.959
host has injected resource content into the conversation, the model reads it as it would

70
00:07:28.959 --> 00:07:35.519
any other context, but it didn't select it. This is the key distinction between resources and tools.

71
00:07:36.079 --> 00:07:42.079
Tools are model controlled. Resources are application controlled. The host selects. The

72
00:07:42.079 --> 00:07:48.320
model reads. Confusing these two control models is the mistake that comes up most often when people

73
00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:54.079
first work with resources. Keep the control points sharp. The application controls resources

74
00:07:54.079 --> 00:08:01.359
and the model controls tool invocations. Resources give a host application an addressable way to pull

75
00:08:01.359 --> 00:08:08.720
server data into model context. That matters because the control points sit with the application,

76
00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:14.559
not the model. The model reads what it's handed and it never goes shopping for a resource on its

77
00:08:14.559 --> 00:08:20.480
own. One thing to watch is that mixing up that control point is the most common misread of the

78
00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:24.320
primitive. That's a wrap on this lesson. I'll see you in the next one.

